window.UNITY_DOCS_INDEX = [{"title": "ATLAS Analysis on Unity", "url": "index.html", "text": "UMass Unity provides the compute resources. CVMFS supplies the ATLAS software. An Enterprise Linux 9 container brings them together on Unity's Ubuntu hosts. \n This guide documents how to use Unity as an analysis facility for ATLAS, from an interactive shell to a reproducible analysis environment. \n \n Start here \n Setting up the environment → \n Request CPU or GPU resources, enter an EL9 container, and load the software needed for your analysis. Includes optional shell shortcuts and checks for common setup problems. \n \n How the environment fits together \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Component \n Its role \n \n \n \n \n Unity login node \n Connect, edit files, and request resources. \n \n \n Slurm allocation \n Reserve CPUs, memory, time, and optional GPUs on a compute node. \n \n \n Apptainer container \n Provide the EL9 userspace required by the ATLAS release. \n \n \n CVMFS \n Deliver centrally maintained software and container images. \n \n \n Your work directory \n Keep analysis code, builds, inputs, and outputs on writable Unity storage. \n \n \n \n Start the container  after  the allocation has started. The container runs within that allocation; it does not reserve additional resources. \n About this guide \n The first chapter covers interactive environment setup. Further chapters will cover data access and authentication, batch processing, analysis workflows, and notebooks and machine learning as those procedures are established. \n The  Unity documentation  is the reference for cluster access, storage, and scheduling. The  ATLAS Software Documentation  is the reference for ATLAS software and analysis procedures. \n \n Initial version.  These instructions combine the documented Unity and ATLAS interfaces. End-to-end validation on Unity's newly installed CVMFS service is still pending. The first chapter includes checks to run on an allocated compute node. \n \n Found a problem or tested a setup?  Open an issue  with the relevant command, error, and compute-node hostname. Omit credentials and tokens. \n"}, {"title": "How the environment fits together", "url": "index.html#how-the-environment-fits-together", "text": "How the environment fits together \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Component \n Its role \n \n \n \n \n Unity login node \n Connect, edit files, and request resources. \n \n \n Slurm allocation \n Reserve CPUs, memory, time, and optional GPUs on a compute node. \n \n \n Apptainer container \n Provide the EL9 userspace required by the ATLAS release. \n \n \n CVMFS \n Deliver centrally maintained software and container images. \n \n \n Your work directory \n Keep analysis code, builds, inputs, and outputs on writable Unity storage. \n \n \n \n Start the container  after  the allocation has started. The container runs within that allocation; it does not reserve additional resources. \n"}, {"title": "About this guide", "url": "index.html#about-this-guide", "text": "About this guide \n The first chapter covers interactive environment setup. Further chapters will cover data access and authentication, batch processing, analysis workflows, and notebooks and machine learning as those procedures are established. \n The  Unity documentation  is the reference for cluster access, storage, and scheduling. The  ATLAS Software Documentation  is the reference for ATLAS software and analysis procedures. \n \n Initial version.  These instructions combine the documented Unity and ATLAS interfaces. End-to-end validation on Unity's newly installed CVMFS service is still pending. The first chapter includes checks to run on an allocated compute node. \n \n Found a problem or tested a setup?  Open an issue  with the relevant command, error, and compute-node hostname. Omit credentials and tokens. \n"}, {"title": "Setting up the environment", "url": "setting-up-environment/index.html", "text": "Use a Slurm allocation for your work, then start an EL9 container on the allocated compute node. Inside it, ATLAS software is available through CVMFS without a local installation. \n \n Before you begin:  you need a Unity account, access to a group work directory, and a Bash shell. CVMFS must be accessible on the  compute node , not just the login node. This first version has not yet been tested end to end on Unity. \n \n 1. Connect to Unity \n From your own computer, connect using your Unity username: \n # Your computer — replace YOUR_UNITY_USERNAME.\nssh YOUR_UNITY_USERNAME@login.unityhpc.org \n Follow Unity's  SSH guide  for account and SSH-key setup. Use the login node to prepare and submit work; run builds, event processing, and training on allocated compute nodes. \n 2. Request compute resources \n Choose  one  of the following commands on the login node. Adjust the resource request to your workload. \n CPU session \n # Unity login node\nsalloc --partition=cpu --nodes=1 --ntasks=1 \\\n       --cpus-per-task=4 --mem=16G --time=02:00:00 \n GPU session \n # Unity login node — alternative to the CPU request\nsalloc --partition=gpu --nodes=1 --ntasks=1 --gpus=1 \\\n       --cpus-per-task=4 --mem=16G --time=02:00:00 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Option \n Meaning in these examples \n \n \n \n \n --nodes=1 --ntasks=1 \n One node and one task. \n \n \n --cpus-per-task=4 \n Four CPU cores for that task. Configure your application's threads accordingly. \n \n \n --mem=16G \n 16 GB of host memory for the node allocation; this is not GPU memory. \n \n \n --time=02:00:00 \n A two-hour wall-time limit. \n \n \n --gpus=1 \n One GPU, only in the GPU example. \n \n \n \n Unity's documented  salloc  workflow opens a shell on the compute node once resources are available. Confirm your location: \n # After the allocation is granted\nhostname -f\nprintf 'Job: %s\\nNodes: %s\\n' \"$SLURM_JOB_ID\" \"$SLURM_JOB_NODELIST\"\nuname -m \n The hostname should be a compute node in your allocation. If you are still on a login node with an active allocation, use  srun --pty bash -l  to start a shell on the allocated node. Do not run a second  salloc  inside the first one. \n This guide targets  x86_64  nodes. Containers do not translate CPU architectures; ARM and POWER partitions need compatible software and images.  gpu-preempt  is an alternative when interruption is acceptable. Consult the current  partition list  and  GPU guide  before choosing constraints or preemptible resources. \n 3. Prepare the host environment \n Run the following in the  Ubuntu compute-node shell , before entering a container: \n module load apptainer/latest\napptainer --version\n\nexport ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE=/cvmfs/atlas.cern.ch/repo/ATLASLocalRootBase\nalias setupATLAS='source ${ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE}/user/atlasLocalSetup.sh'\n\nls -l \"$ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE/user/atlasLocalSetup.sh\" \n The last command must find the setup script. Stop here if it fails: an empty  /cvmfs  directory alone does not establish that the ATLAS repository is available. Report the compute-node hostname and the missing path to Unity support. The first access to a CVMFS path may take longer while content is fetched. \n Start from a clean host shell. Deactivate any Conda or Python virtual environment and avoid loading Ubuntu ROOT, Python, or compiler modules for use inside the EL9 environment. Load the Apptainer runtime on the host; select analysis tools inside the container. \n Choose a work directory \n Replace  pi_YOUR_PI  with your actual Unity group directory. Do not run the placeholder unchanged. \n # Ubuntu compute node — edit the group directory first\nexport ATLAS_WORKDIR=\"/work/pi_YOUR_PI/$USER/atlas\"\nmkdir -p \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR\"\ncd \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR\"\npwd -P \n Use  /work  or a workspace allocated through Unity's  scratch service  for active job I/O. Keep shell configuration in your home directory. Consult the  storage guide  for quotas, retention, and moving data from longer-term storage. \n 4. Enter the EL9 container \n For a CPU session, run: \n # Ubuntu compute node, in your analysis directory\nsetupATLAS -c el9 -m /work \n -c el9  selects the ATLAS EL9 environment.  -m /work  makes Unity's work filesystem visible inside it. File permissions still apply. If your work is on scratch instead, use  -m /scratch ; for other storage, bind the relevant host path. Check  setupATLAS -c -h  for additional mount options supported by the installed launcher. \n The launcher initializes the ATLAS user interface in the container. Check it before selecting a release: \n # Inside the EL9 container\ncat /etc/os-release\nuname -m\npwd\ntype lsetup\ntype asetup\nls /cvmfs/atlas.cern.ch/repo\nls /work \n Expect an EL9 distribution and  x86_64 . A container shares the host kernel, so  uname -r  is  not  a test of its Linux distribution. The launcher may map the working directory to  /srv  and use a different home path. Check  pwd ; your  /work/...  directory should also be accessible through the explicit mount. Host variables such as  ATLAS_WORKDIR  need not be preserved. \n If  lsetup  is unavailable despite a successful container launch, initialize the interface explicitly  inside  that container: \n export ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE=/cvmfs/atlas.cern.ch/repo/ATLASLocalRootBase\nsource \"$ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE/user/atlasLocalSetup.sh\" \n \n Two separate choices.  The container selects the operating-system environment.  asetup  selects an ATLAS software release. Entering EL9 does not by itself load AnalysisBase, AthAnalysis, or Athena. \n \n 5. Load analysis software \n Choose the environment needed by your analysis. Use a fresh container session when switching between unrelated software stacks. \n A standalone ROOT check \n In an EL9 session with no analysis release loaded: \n lsetup root\nroot-config --version\npython -c 'import ROOT; print(ROOT.gROOT.GetVersion())' \n This checks a basic ROOT/PyROOT environment. For reproducible work, select an explicit tool version using the choices shown by  lsetup \"root --help\" , and record it with your analysis. \n AnalysisBase, AthAnalysis, or Athena \n For an analysis release, begin in a  fresh EL9 session  and select the exact project and version required by your analysis, following the ATLAS  release setup guide . For example, this prompts for a version instead of silently choosing a moving default: \n # Inside a fresh EL9 container, in your analysis directory\nread -r -p 'AnalysisBase release required by your analysis: ' ATLAS_RELEASE\nif [[ -n \"$ATLAS_RELEASE\" ]]; then\n    asetup \"AnalysisBase,${ATLAS_RELEASE}\"\nfi \n Use  AthAnalysis  or  Athena  in place of  AnalysisBase  when that is what your workflow requires. Check the setup output for a successful release selection and an EL9-compatible platform. Do not guess a release number from this guide; the analysis determines the supported release. \n After a successful setup: \n printf 'Project: %s\\nVersion: %s\\nPlatform: %s\\n' \\\n       \"$AtlasProject\" \"$AtlasVersion\" \"$BINARY_TAG\"\ncommand -v python\nroot-config --version\npython -c 'import ROOT; print(ROOT.gROOT.GetVersion())' \n Use the ROOT and Python supplied by the release. Keep standalone  lsetup root  and release setup in separate sessions. Record the project, version, platform, and container information with your code; record the resolved image from the launcher output if you need to reproduce the precise container as well. \n 6. Enable GPU access \n For an  NVIDIA GPU allocation , use this launch sequence in place of the CPU container command. First check the host: \n # Ubuntu GPU compute node, after salloc and host preparation\nnvidia-smi\nprintf 'CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=%s\\n' \"${CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES:-<unset>}\" \n Apptainer's  APPTAINER_NV  enables NVIDIA device and driver-library passthrough, corresponding to  --nv . Pass Slurm's device selection into the clean container environment as well: \n # Ubuntu GPU compute node\nexport APPTAINER_NV=true\nif [[ -n ${CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES:-} ]]; then\n    export APPTAINERENV_CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=\"$CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES\"\nfi\nsetupATLAS -c el9 -m /work \n Then check  inside  the container: \n nvidia-smi\nprintf 'CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=%s\\n' \"${CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES:-<unset>}\" \n Check the launcher's output to confirm it uses Apptainer. If it selects a different runtime or the GPU check fails, inspect  setupATLAS -c -h  and the  Apptainer GPU documentation  before proceeding. This combined ATLAS/Unity GPU path still needs validation on Unity. \n GPU visibility does not install a CUDA-enabled analysis framework, or make ordinary ATLAS code use the GPU. A compatible GPU software stack is a separate choice. Do not override Slurm's device selection or set it to  all ;  nvidia-smi  may enumerate more devices than your application is assigned. Keep these GPU exports out of your general  .bashrc  and remove them in the host shell after leaving the container: \n # Back in the Ubuntu compute-node shell after exiting the container\nunset APPTAINER_NV APPTAINERENV_CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES \n 7. Add optional shell shortcuts \n Add the following to  your existing   ~/.bashrc  on Unity. These lines define commands; they do not allocate resources or start software at login. The same snippet is available as  atlas-bashrc.sh . \n export ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE=/cvmfs/atlas.cern.ch/repo/ATLASLocalRootBase\n\n# Shortcuts for interactive Bash sessions only.\nif [[ $- == *i* ]]; then\n    alias setupATLAS='source ${ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE}/user/atlasLocalSetup.sh'\n    alias unity_cpu='salloc --partition=cpu --nodes=1 --ntasks=1 --cpus-per-task=4 --mem=16G --time=02:00:00'\n    alias unity_gpu='salloc --partition=gpu --nodes=1 --ntasks=1 --gpus=1 --cpus-per-task=4 --mem=16G --time=02:00:00'\nfi \n Reload it in the  host shell  with  source ~/.bashrc . You can then use  unity_cpu  or  unity_gpu  on the login node, followed by the host preparation and container commands on the allocated node. \n You may also add your edited  ATLAS_WORKDIR  export. Keep  module load ,  setupATLAS -c el9 ,  asetup , and  lsetup  as explicit commands rather than executing them automatically from  .bashrc . This avoids unexpected containers or incompatible software environments when starting new shells. In scripts, source  atlasLocalSetup.sh  explicitly; non-interactive Bash does not normally expand aliases. \n 8. End or reconnect to a session \n Run  exit  inside the container to return to the Ubuntu compute-node shell. Run  exit  again to leave the interactive allocation and release its resources. A new allocation requires a new container and software setup; files in your work directory remain. \n For a session that survives an SSH disconnect, start  tmux new -s atlas   on the login node before  salloc . Detach with  Ctrl+B , then  D . Reconnect to the same login node and use  tmux attach -t atlas . Record that login hostname before starting.  tmux  does not extend the Slurm wall-time limit or prevent preemption. See Unity's  interactive-job guidance . \n Troubleshooting \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Symptom \n What to check \n \n \n \n \n setupATLAS: command not found \n Define the export and alias in the current Bash shell, or source the setup script explicitly. \n \n \n Missing  atlasLocalSetup.sh \n Test its full CVMFS path on the allocated compute node; ask Unity support if unavailable. \n \n \n apptainer: command not found \n Load  apptainer/latest  on the host. Use  module avail apptainer  if the module name changes. \n \n \n Container fails with namespace or mount errors \n Save the launcher output and hostname for support; node/runtime configuration may need adjustment. \n \n \n Work files disappear inside the container \n Add the correct host path with  -m , including a symlink's real target; verify with  pwd -P  before launch. \n \n \n Container still reports Ubuntu \n Check  /etc/os-release ; the container may have failed to start, leaving you in the host shell. \n \n \n ROOT/Python errors after setup \n Try a fresh container, choose one software stack, and check the release's platform. \n \n \n GPU works on host but not in container \n Confirm an NVIDIA allocation, Apptainer passthrough, and preservation of Slurm's device selection. \n \n \n Allocation is pending \n Inspect  squeue -u \"$USER\"  on the host; reduce an unnecessarily large request or consult current partition availability. \n \n \n \n References \n \n ATLAS Software Documentation :  environment prerequisites  and  user-interface setup . \n Unity documentation :  interactive CLI jobs ,  container support ,  partitions , and  storage . \n US ATLAS Analysis Facilities: Apptainer usage . \n Apptainer GPU support  and  environment-variable reference . \n \n Documentation reviewed:  7 November 2026 . Unity runtime validation:  pending . \n"}, {"title": "1. Connect to Unity", "url": "setting-up-environment/index.html#connect", "text": "1. Connect to Unity \n From your own computer, connect using your Unity username: \n # Your computer — replace YOUR_UNITY_USERNAME.\nssh YOUR_UNITY_USERNAME@login.unityhpc.org \n Follow Unity's  SSH guide  for account and SSH-key setup. Use the login node to prepare and submit work; run builds, event processing, and training on allocated compute nodes. \n"}, {"title": "2. Request compute resources", "url": "setting-up-environment/index.html#allocate", "text": "2. Request compute resources \n Choose  one  of the following commands on the login node. Adjust the resource request to your workload. \n CPU session \n # Unity login node\nsalloc --partition=cpu --nodes=1 --ntasks=1 \\\n       --cpus-per-task=4 --mem=16G --time=02:00:00 \n GPU session \n # Unity login node — alternative to the CPU request\nsalloc --partition=gpu --nodes=1 --ntasks=1 --gpus=1 \\\n       --cpus-per-task=4 --mem=16G --time=02:00:00 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Option \n Meaning in these examples \n \n \n \n \n --nodes=1 --ntasks=1 \n One node and one task. \n \n \n --cpus-per-task=4 \n Four CPU cores for that task. Configure your application's threads accordingly. \n \n \n --mem=16G \n 16 GB of host memory for the node allocation; this is not GPU memory. \n \n \n --time=02:00:00 \n A two-hour wall-time limit. \n \n \n --gpus=1 \n One GPU, only in the GPU example. \n \n \n \n Unity's documented  salloc  workflow opens a shell on the compute node once resources are available. Confirm your location: \n # After the allocation is granted\nhostname -f\nprintf 'Job: %s\\nNodes: %s\\n' \"$SLURM_JOB_ID\" \"$SLURM_JOB_NODELIST\"\nuname -m \n The hostname should be a compute node in your allocation. If you are still on a login node with an active allocation, use  srun --pty bash -l  to start a shell on the allocated node. Do not run a second  salloc  inside the first one. \n This guide targets  x86_64  nodes. Containers do not translate CPU architectures; ARM and POWER partitions need compatible software and images.  gpu-preempt  is an alternative when interruption is acceptable. Consult the current  partition list  and  GPU guide  before choosing constraints or preemptible resources. \n"}, {"title": "3. Prepare the host environment", "url": "setting-up-environment/index.html#prepare-host", "text": "3. Prepare the host environment \n Run the following in the  Ubuntu compute-node shell , before entering a container: \n module load apptainer/latest\napptainer --version\n\nexport ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE=/cvmfs/atlas.cern.ch/repo/ATLASLocalRootBase\nalias setupATLAS='source ${ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE}/user/atlasLocalSetup.sh'\n\nls -l \"$ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE/user/atlasLocalSetup.sh\" \n The last command must find the setup script. Stop here if it fails: an empty  /cvmfs  directory alone does not establish that the ATLAS repository is available. Report the compute-node hostname and the missing path to Unity support. The first access to a CVMFS path may take longer while content is fetched. \n Start from a clean host shell. Deactivate any Conda or Python virtual environment and avoid loading Ubuntu ROOT, Python, or compiler modules for use inside the EL9 environment. Load the Apptainer runtime on the host; select analysis tools inside the container. \n Choose a work directory \n Replace  pi_YOUR_PI  with your actual Unity group directory. Do not run the placeholder unchanged. \n # Ubuntu compute node — edit the group directory first\nexport ATLAS_WORKDIR=\"/work/pi_YOUR_PI/$USER/atlas\"\nmkdir -p \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR\"\ncd \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR\"\npwd -P \n Use  /work  or a workspace allocated through Unity's  scratch service  for active job I/O. Keep shell configuration in your home directory. Consult the  storage guide  for quotas, retention, and moving data from longer-term storage. \n"}, {"title": "4. Enter the EL9 container", "url": "setting-up-environment/index.html#container", "text": "4. Enter the EL9 container \n For a CPU session, run: \n # Ubuntu compute node, in your analysis directory\nsetupATLAS -c el9 -m /work \n -c el9  selects the ATLAS EL9 environment.  -m /work  makes Unity's work filesystem visible inside it. File permissions still apply. If your work is on scratch instead, use  -m /scratch ; for other storage, bind the relevant host path. Check  setupATLAS -c -h  for additional mount options supported by the installed launcher. \n The launcher initializes the ATLAS user interface in the container. Check it before selecting a release: \n # Inside the EL9 container\ncat /etc/os-release\nuname -m\npwd\ntype lsetup\ntype asetup\nls /cvmfs/atlas.cern.ch/repo\nls /work \n Expect an EL9 distribution and  x86_64 . A container shares the host kernel, so  uname -r  is  not  a test of its Linux distribution. The launcher may map the working directory to  /srv  and use a different home path. Check  pwd ; your  /work/...  directory should also be accessible through the explicit mount. Host variables such as  ATLAS_WORKDIR  need not be preserved. \n If  lsetup  is unavailable despite a successful container launch, initialize the interface explicitly  inside  that container: \n export ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE=/cvmfs/atlas.cern.ch/repo/ATLASLocalRootBase\nsource \"$ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE/user/atlasLocalSetup.sh\" \n \n Two separate choices.  The container selects the operating-system environment.  asetup  selects an ATLAS software release. Entering EL9 does not by itself load AnalysisBase, AthAnalysis, or Athena. \n \n"}, {"title": "5. Load analysis software", "url": "setting-up-environment/index.html#software", "text": "5. Load analysis software \n Choose the environment needed by your analysis. Use a fresh container session when switching between unrelated software stacks. \n A standalone ROOT check \n In an EL9 session with no analysis release loaded: \n lsetup root\nroot-config --version\npython -c 'import ROOT; print(ROOT.gROOT.GetVersion())' \n This checks a basic ROOT/PyROOT environment. For reproducible work, select an explicit tool version using the choices shown by  lsetup \"root --help\" , and record it with your analysis. \n AnalysisBase, AthAnalysis, or Athena \n For an analysis release, begin in a  fresh EL9 session  and select the exact project and version required by your analysis, following the ATLAS  release setup guide . For example, this prompts for a version instead of silently choosing a moving default: \n # Inside a fresh EL9 container, in your analysis directory\nread -r -p 'AnalysisBase release required by your analysis: ' ATLAS_RELEASE\nif [[ -n \"$ATLAS_RELEASE\" ]]; then\n    asetup \"AnalysisBase,${ATLAS_RELEASE}\"\nfi \n Use  AthAnalysis  or  Athena  in place of  AnalysisBase  when that is what your workflow requires. Check the setup output for a successful release selection and an EL9-compatible platform. Do not guess a release number from this guide; the analysis determines the supported release. \n After a successful setup: \n printf 'Project: %s\\nVersion: %s\\nPlatform: %s\\n' \\\n       \"$AtlasProject\" \"$AtlasVersion\" \"$BINARY_TAG\"\ncommand -v python\nroot-config --version\npython -c 'import ROOT; print(ROOT.gROOT.GetVersion())' \n Use the ROOT and Python supplied by the release. Keep standalone  lsetup root  and release setup in separate sessions. Record the project, version, platform, and container information with your code; record the resolved image from the launcher output if you need to reproduce the precise container as well. \n"}, {"title": "6. Enable GPU access", "url": "setting-up-environment/index.html#gpu", "text": "6. Enable GPU access \n For an  NVIDIA GPU allocation , use this launch sequence in place of the CPU container command. First check the host: \n # Ubuntu GPU compute node, after salloc and host preparation\nnvidia-smi\nprintf 'CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=%s\\n' \"${CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES:-<unset>}\" \n Apptainer's  APPTAINER_NV  enables NVIDIA device and driver-library passthrough, corresponding to  --nv . Pass Slurm's device selection into the clean container environment as well: \n # Ubuntu GPU compute node\nexport APPTAINER_NV=true\nif [[ -n ${CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES:-} ]]; then\n    export APPTAINERENV_CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=\"$CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES\"\nfi\nsetupATLAS -c el9 -m /work \n Then check  inside  the container: \n nvidia-smi\nprintf 'CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=%s\\n' \"${CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES:-<unset>}\" \n Check the launcher's output to confirm it uses Apptainer. If it selects a different runtime or the GPU check fails, inspect  setupATLAS -c -h  and the  Apptainer GPU documentation  before proceeding. This combined ATLAS/Unity GPU path still needs validation on Unity. \n GPU visibility does not install a CUDA-enabled analysis framework, or make ordinary ATLAS code use the GPU. A compatible GPU software stack is a separate choice. Do not override Slurm's device selection or set it to  all ;  nvidia-smi  may enumerate more devices than your application is assigned. Keep these GPU exports out of your general  .bashrc  and remove them in the host shell after leaving the container: \n # Back in the Ubuntu compute-node shell after exiting the container\nunset APPTAINER_NV APPTAINERENV_CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES \n"}, {"title": "7. Add optional shell shortcuts", "url": "setting-up-environment/index.html#bashrc", "text": "7. Add optional shell shortcuts \n Add the following to  your existing   ~/.bashrc  on Unity. These lines define commands; they do not allocate resources or start software at login. The same snippet is available as  atlas-bashrc.sh . \n export ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE=/cvmfs/atlas.cern.ch/repo/ATLASLocalRootBase\n\n# Shortcuts for interactive Bash sessions only.\nif [[ $- == *i* ]]; then\n    alias setupATLAS='source ${ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE}/user/atlasLocalSetup.sh'\n    alias unity_cpu='salloc --partition=cpu --nodes=1 --ntasks=1 --cpus-per-task=4 --mem=16G --time=02:00:00'\n    alias unity_gpu='salloc --partition=gpu --nodes=1 --ntasks=1 --gpus=1 --cpus-per-task=4 --mem=16G --time=02:00:00'\nfi \n Reload it in the  host shell  with  source ~/.bashrc . You can then use  unity_cpu  or  unity_gpu  on the login node, followed by the host preparation and container commands on the allocated node. \n You may also add your edited  ATLAS_WORKDIR  export. Keep  module load ,  setupATLAS -c el9 ,  asetup , and  lsetup  as explicit commands rather than executing them automatically from  .bashrc . This avoids unexpected containers or incompatible software environments when starting new shells. In scripts, source  atlasLocalSetup.sh  explicitly; non-interactive Bash does not normally expand aliases. \n"}, {"title": "8. End or reconnect to a session", "url": "setting-up-environment/index.html#sessions", "text": "8. End or reconnect to a session \n Run  exit  inside the container to return to the Ubuntu compute-node shell. Run  exit  again to leave the interactive allocation and release its resources. A new allocation requires a new container and software setup; files in your work directory remain. \n For a session that survives an SSH disconnect, start  tmux new -s atlas   on the login node before  salloc . Detach with  Ctrl+B , then  D . Reconnect to the same login node and use  tmux attach -t atlas . Record that login hostname before starting.  tmux  does not extend the Slurm wall-time limit or prevent preemption. See Unity's  interactive-job guidance . \n"}, {"title": "Troubleshooting", "url": "setting-up-environment/index.html#troubleshooting", "text": "Troubleshooting \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Symptom \n What to check \n \n \n \n \n setupATLAS: command not found \n Define the export and alias in the current Bash shell, or source the setup script explicitly. \n \n \n Missing  atlasLocalSetup.sh \n Test its full CVMFS path on the allocated compute node; ask Unity support if unavailable. \n \n \n apptainer: command not found \n Load  apptainer/latest  on the host. Use  module avail apptainer  if the module name changes. \n \n \n Container fails with namespace or mount errors \n Save the launcher output and hostname for support; node/runtime configuration may need adjustment. \n \n \n Work files disappear inside the container \n Add the correct host path with  -m , including a symlink's real target; verify with  pwd -P  before launch. \n \n \n Container still reports Ubuntu \n Check  /etc/os-release ; the container may have failed to start, leaving you in the host shell. \n \n \n ROOT/Python errors after setup \n Try a fresh container, choose one software stack, and check the release's platform. \n \n \n GPU works on host but not in container \n Confirm an NVIDIA allocation, Apptainer passthrough, and preservation of Slurm's device selection. \n \n \n Allocation is pending \n Inspect  squeue -u \"$USER\"  on the host; reduce an unnecessarily large request or consult current partition availability. \n \n \n \n"}, {"title": "References", "url": "setting-up-environment/index.html#references", "text": "References \n \n ATLAS Software Documentation :  environment prerequisites  and  user-interface setup . \n Unity documentation :  interactive CLI jobs ,  container support ,  partitions , and  storage . \n US ATLAS Analysis Facilities: Apptainer usage . \n Apptainer GPU support  and  environment-variable reference . \n \n Documentation reviewed:  7 November 2026 . Unity runtime validation:  pending . \n"}];
